\"memberOf\":\"Part of the {parent} low emission zone\",\"factBadge\":\"Badge\",\"factSince\":\"Green badge since\",\"factArea\":\"Area\",\"factFine\":\"Fine\",\"green\":\"Green\",\"areaHeading\":\"Where the low emission zone applies\",\"areaMemberNote\":\"Not the whole city lies inside the low emission zone; it covers designated areas within the city.\",\"areaMapIsParent\":\"The map shows the entire {parent} low emission zone, not just the {city} part.\",\"carveOutsHeading\":\"Not part of the low emission zone\",\"autobahnHeading\":\"Motorways are exempt\",\"autobahnBody\":\"Motorways are not covered by low emission zone bans and may be driven without a badge, including where they run straight through a low emission zone.\",\"vehiclesHeading\":\"Which vehicles may enter\",\"vehiclesLead\":\"The classification is the same across Germany. It follows the emission key number in the registration certificate (Zulassungsbescheinigung Teil I, field 14.1), not the age of the vehicle.\",\"colGroup\":\"Pollutant group\",\"colBadge\":\"Badge\",\"colDiesel\":\"Diesel\",\"colPetrol\":\"Petrol\",\"badgeNone\":\"none\",\"badgeRed\":\"red\",\"badgeYellow\":\"yellow\",\"badgeGreen\":\"green\",\"petrolNote\":\"Petrol cars have only two classifications: the green badge with a regulated catalytic converter, and none at all without one. There is no yellow or red badge for petrol cars.\",\"foreignHeading\":\"Vehicles registered abroad\",\"foreignLead\":\"Vehicles registered abroad need a badge too. If the Euro standard is not stated in the vehicle documents, the classification is based on the date of first registration.\",\"colEuro\":\"Euro standard\",\"colFirstReg\":\"First registration\",\"dieselHeading\":\"Additional diesel driving ban\",\"dieselWarning\":\"A green badge is not enough here.\",\"dieselSince\":\"In force since {date}\",\"dieselStreetsHeading\":\"Affected road sections\",\"dieselExemptionsHeading\":\"Exempt routes\",\"exemptionsHeading\":\"Exemptions\",\"exemptionsNationalLead\":\"Under Annex 3 of the 35th BImSchV, the following vehicles may enter any German low emission zone without a badge:\",\"exemptionsLandHeading\":\"Individual exemptions in {land}\",\"exemptionsLandGap\":\"The criteria for individual exemptions are set by each federal state. We are currently checking them against the official source and will publish them only once that is done.\",\"badgeOfficeHeading\":\"Getting a badge locally\",\"fineHeading\":\"Fine and enforcement\",\"fineAmount\":\"€{amount}\",\"fineTotal\":\"With fees and costs, €{total} in total.\",\"finePoints\":\"No penalty points, no driving ban.\",\"fineParking\":\"The fine also applies to parking in the low emission zone, not just to driving into it.\",\"enforcementHeading\":\"How it is enforced\",\"enforcementNoAnpr\":\"There are no entry barriers and no number plate cameras. What gets checked is the badge behind the windscreen.\",\"getBadgeHeading\":\"Buying a badge\",\"getBadgeIssuers\":\"Issuing points\",\"getBadgePrice\":\"Cost\",\"getBadgeValidity\":\"Validity\",\"getBadgeAbroad\":\"Ordering from abroad\",\"getBadgeShop\":\"Online shop of the Berlin vehicle registration office (also in English)\",\"stillAppliesHeading\":\"What still applies\",\"nothingAppliesBody\":\"There is no emissions-based access restriction. You do not need a green badge here.\",\"whyRevokedHeading\":\"Why the low emission zone was lifted\",\"whyRevokedBody\":\"Since April 2019, section 47 of the Federal Immission Control Act has provided that driving bans are, as a rule, neither necessary nor proportionate in areas where the annual mean for nitrogen dioxide does not exceed 50 µg/m³. On that basis, 19 low emission zones have been lifted since 2020.\",\"formerZoneHeading\":\"The former low emission zone\",\"formerZoneBody\":\"The zone existed from {from} to {to} and covered around {area} km².\",\"whereStillNeededHeading\":\"Where you still need a badge\",\"metaTitleHub\":\"Low emission zones in Germany: all {active} zones \u0026 badge\",\"metaDescHub\":\"All {active} active low emission zones in Germany: which badge your car gets, where they apply, what a violation costs and which {gone} zones are gone. With a map.\",\"hubEyebrow\":\"Low emission zones · Germany\",\"hubH1\":\"Low emission zones in Germany\",\"hubLead\":\"{active} zones, one badge: the green badge (Umweltplakette) is valid across Germany, costs around €5 once and stays valid for the life of the vehicle. Here is which badge your car gets, where you need it and what a violation costs.\",\"hubVerdict\":\"The same rule applies in all {active} active low emission zones: entry only with a green badge, around the clock, 365 days a year.\",\"hubVerdictFacts\":\"Fine €100 · Badge from €5 · Valid indefinitely\",\"hubOnThisPage\":\"On this page\",\"hubNavBadge\":\"Which badge?\",\"hubNavForeign\":\"Foreign vehicles\",\"hubNavBuy\":\"Buying a badge\",\"hubNavZones\":\"All zones\",\"hubNavFine\":\"Fine\",\"hubNavExemptions\":\"Exemptions\",\"hubNavDiesel\":\"Diesel bans\",\"hubNavRevoked\":\"Lifted zones\",\"hubNavFaq\":\"Common questions\",\"hubBadgeHeading\":\"Which badge does my car get?\",\"hubForeignShopNote\":\"Berlin recommends ordering the badge around three weeks before your trip. The online shop of the Berlin vehicle registration office accepts foreign vehicle documents and ships abroad.\",\"hubZonesHeading\":\"Where you need the badge\",\"hubZonesLead\":\"All {active} active low emission zones, grouped by federal state. Each one applies around the clock and requires the green badge; three cities also ban older diesels.\",\"hubFeaturedBerlin\":\"The city centre inside the S-Bahn ring, around 88 km².\",\"hubFeaturedMuenchen\":\"The Mittlerer Ring and everything inside it. Plus a diesel ban from Euro 4/IV down.\",\"hubFeaturedStuttgart\":\"The entire city area. Plus a diesel ban, up to Euro 5/V in the Talkessel basin.\",\"hubFeaturedRuhr\":\"Europe's largest low emission zone: 866 km² across 13 cities in the Ruhr area.\",\"hubDieselChip\":\"Diesel ban\",\"hubZoneCount\":\"{count} zones\",\"hubZoneCountOne\":\"1 zone\",\"hubDieselHeading\":\"Where the green badge is not enough\",\"hubDieselLead\":\"Three cities also ban older diesels. There it is the Euro standard in the vehicle documents that counts, not the badge on the windscreen.\",\"hubDieselStuttgart\":\"Diesels up to Euro 4/IV banned across the whole city area, and up to Euro 5/V in the small low emission zone.\",\"hubDieselMuenchen\":\"Diesels up to Euro 4/IV banned, including the Mittlerer Ring. Residents and delivery traffic are exempt.\",\"hubDieselDarmstadt\":\"Two road sections: diesels up to Euro 5 and petrol cars up to Euro 2 are banned.\",\"hubMotoLead\":\"Motorcycles, mopeds and trikes never need a badge. Annex 3 of the 35th BImSchV also exempts, across Germany:\",\"hubRevokedHeading\":\"These low emission zones no longer exist\",\"hubRevokedLead\":\"{revoked} low emission zones have been lifted since 2020, most recently Freiburg and Mainz. The reason: once the annual mean for nitrogen dioxide stays below 50 µg/m³, driving bans count as disproportionate under section 47 of the Federal Immission Control Act (BImSchG).\",\"hubRevokedOn\":\"lifted {date}\",\"hubNeverHad\":\"never had a low emission zone\",\"hubFaqQ1\":\"Which badge do I need for German low emission zones?\",\"hubFaqA1\":\"In all {active} active low emission zones the rule is the same: entry only with a green badge (pollutant group 4). Yellow and red badges no longer allow entry anywhere.\",\"hubFaqQ2\":\"What does the green badge cost?\",\"hubFaqA2\":\"Around €5 at the vehicle registration office, around €6 online including postage. TÜV, DEKRA and emissions-test (AU) authorised workshops issue it as well; private sellers sometimes charge considerably more.\",\"hubFaqQ3\":\"How long is the badge valid?\",\"hubFaqA3\":\"Indefinitely. You only need a new one if the number plate changes, the windscreen is replaced or the badge has become unreadable.\",\"hubFaqQ4\":\"What does driving in without a badge cost?\",\"hubFaqA4\":\"A fine of €100, or €128.50 in total with fees and costs. There are no penalty points. The fine also applies to parking in the zone.\",\"hubFaqQ5\":\"Do motorcycles need a green badge?\",\"hubFaqA5\":\"No. Two- and three-wheeled motor vehicles are exempt from the badge requirement across Germany.\",\"hubFaqQ6\":\"Does the low emission zone apply on the motorway?\",\"hubFaqA6\":\"No. Motorways are exempt from low emission zone bans and may be driven without a badge, even where they pass through a zone.\",\"hubFaqQ7\":\"Do foreign vehicles need a badge?\",\"hubFaqA7\":\"Yes. Vehicles registered abroad need the green badge too. Berlin recommends ordering it online around three weeks before your trip; on the spot, TÜV and DEKRA issue it immediately.\",\"hubFaqQ8\":\"Is the green badge enough everywhere?\",\"hubFaqA8\":\"Almost everywhere. Stuttgart and Munich also ban diesels up to Euro 4/IV, and Darmstadt closes two road sections. There the Euro standard decides, not the badge.\",\"membersHeading\":\"Cities in this low emission zone\",\"membersLead\":\"The low emission zone covers designated areas in {count} municipalities.\",\"relatedHeading\":\"Other low emission zones in {land}\",\"allZonesHeading\":\"All low emission zones in Germany\",\"officialHeading\":\"Official sources\",\"faqHeading\":\"Common questions\",\"unknownTitle\":\"Not yet officially confirmed\",\"unknownBody\":\"We have not yet checked this detail against an official source. We will publish it only once it is confirmed.\",\"unknownLink\":\"Go to the official page\",\"disclaimer\":\"Information provided without guarantee. The signs on site and the official publication of the responsible authority always take precedence.\",\"sourceUpdated\":\"As of {date}\",\"verifiedOn\":\"Verified by us on {date}\"},\"pricingPage\":{\"title\":\"Plans \u0026 Pricing\",\"subtitle\":\"One app for every speed camera, enforcement zone and road hazard 